# Untitled

## Porphyrin-based framework materials for energy conversion
Jiawei Gu\(^{1}\), Yi Peng\(^{1}\), Ting Zhou\(^{1}\), Jiao Ma\(^{1}\), Huan Pang\(^{1}\) (✉), and Yusuke Yamauchi\(^{2}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\) Country School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou 225009, China  \(^{2}\) Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) and School of Chemical Engineering, The University of Queensland, Brisbane, Queensland 4072, Australia
## ABSTRACT
With the increasing demand for fuel causing serious environmental pollution, it is urgent to develop new and environmentally friendly energy conversion devices. These energy conversion devices, however, require good, inexpensive materials for electrodes and so on. The multifunctional properties of porphyrins enable framework materials (e.g., metal-organic frameworks and covalent organic frameworks) to be applied in energy conversion devices due to their simple synthesis, high chemical stability, abundant metallic active sites, adjustable crystalline structure and high specific surface area. Herein, the types of porphyrin structural blocks are briefly reviewed. They can be used as organic ligands or directly assembled with framework materials to generate high-performance electro-/photo-catalysts. These types of catalysts applied in electro-/photo-catalytic water splitting, electro-/photo-catalytic carbon dioxide reduction, and electrocatalytic oxygen reduction are also summarized and introduced. At the end of the article, we present the challenges of porphyrin-based framework materials in the above application and corresponding solutions. We expect porphyrin-based framework materials to flourish energy conversion in the coming years.
## KEYWORDS
porphyrins, metal-organic frameworks, covalent organic frameworks, energy conversion, electrocatalysis, photocatalysis
## 1 Introduction
Global energy consumption has been multiplying at an exponential rate in the last few decades due to modern agriculture's and industry's demands [1]. It is expected to increase by more than 50% in 2040 [2–5]. At present, 80% of the world's consumable energy comes from fossil fuels, which directly and indirectly leads to the serious energy crises and global warming [6]. To alleviate the rapid consumption of fossil fuels and severe environmental pollution, scientists are looking for novel energy conversion (EC) technologies that can transform sustainable renewable energy (e.g., wind and solar) into transportable and stable chemical energy [7–11]. In this case, high-performance energy conversion technologies based on electrocatalysis and photocatalysis have been studied extensively with unprecedented enthusiasm in recent years because they have great potential for developing sustainable energy conversion technologies to power society in the future. Electrocatalysis plays a vital role in EC systems (e.g., water splitting, carbon dioxide electrocatalytic reduction, and fuel cells [12]). Photocatalysis, which includes light collection, electron–hole separation, and surface redox processes, can produce diversified molecules, for instance, hydrogen and oxygen through the water splitting, and carbon fuels through the reduction of carbon dioxide [13, 14]. Despite extensive study and some achievements in recent years, however, there are still some existing difficulties in EC technologies. For the conventional electrocatalyst: (1) There is usually a large overpotential and slow kinetics, which requires a large amount of additional energy consumption. (2) The short life and high cost of catalysts also greatly hinder their widely used in practical applications [12]. For the conventional photocatalyst: (1) Due to insufficient light collection device, photocatalyst has a narrow absorption zone and low light utilization rate. (2) Long carrier transport distance often leads to serious electron–hole recombination and low photocatalytic efficiency. (3) The composite photocatalyst synthesized by complex pathways has low porosity, low exposure to active sites and poor stability [13]. To address the above limitations, the development of electro/ photo-catalyst materials of new concept with well-behaved, inexpensive, and eco-friendly properties is the most pressing need in the area of EC technologies [15–17].
