In recent years, the study of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) has become one of the most active research fields in the field of materials and chemistry, and a large number of articles have been published in some top journals [18–23]. MOFs are organic-inorganic hybrid materials consisting of the self-assembly of organic ligands and metal ions or clusters through coordination bonds, which are mainly self-assembled into coordination
materials with periodic structures [24–26]. Of course, with the further study of MOFs in recent decades, bond cooperation between MOFs has not only referred to coordination bond interaction, but also includes other interactions, such as hydrogen bonds. While COFs are kinds of porous organic crystalline materials connected by light elements (C, O, N, B, etc.) through covalent bonds, which benefit from the rich designability of organic monomers [27, 28]. The concept of MOFs was first proposed by Yaghi et al. in 1995 [29], and the COFs were first synthesized by Yaghi et al. in 2005 [30]. Furthermore, MOFs are also termed porous coordination polymers (PCPs) by Prof. Susumu Kitagawa [31]. Both MOFs and COFs possess some advantages of high porosity [32–35], adjustable pores [36–39], well-defined structures [40–42], and functional skeletons [43–45]. MOFs and COFs have a wide range of applications in energy storage [46–50], energy conversion [51–59], sensors [60–63], and other fields [64, 65]. Moreover, the organic ligands play a crucial part in the electrochemical performance of MOFs. By adjusting ligand structure or using high-performance ligands, the catalytic activity and chemical stability of MOFs and COFs can be improved [66–70].
Porphyrins are macromolecular heterocyclic materials by porphin \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{20}\mathrm{H}_{14}\mathrm{N}_{4}\right)\) in the middle or \(\upbeta\) position replaced by various functional groups [71–73]. Freebase porphyrins can coordinate with many metal ions or secondary building units (SBUs) in the porphyrin center to form metallic composites, also known as metalloporphyrins. Porphyrins are the active center of many biological enzymes and are important environmentally friendly bionic catalysts with high catalytic efficiency and selectivity. Compared with other structures, metalloporphyrin structures have unique electronic structure, suitable for catalyzing a variety of reactions, so they have higher activity. Structure diagrams of porphyrin core, porphyrins and metalloporphyrins are shown in Scheme 1. With the development of the synthesis of porphyrins and metalloporphyrins, they play an increasingly important role and apply in biomimetic chemistry [74, 75], catalysis [76, 77], solar energy utilization [78, 79], medicine [80], and so on.
However, the porphyrin electro-/photo-catalyst was unstable, easy to deactivate, and difficult to recycle, which limited the further development of EC technologies. Therefore, the scientists envisioned the integration of porphyrins as organic ligands into controllable, highly designed, and structurally stable framework materials such as MOFs and COFs to create an excellent performance of the electro-/photo-catalyst [81–83]. The MOFs and COFs that directly coordinate porphyrins with metal ions or SBUs are called metal porphyrin frameworks (MPFs) and covalent porphyrin frameworks (CPFs). On this basis, related composites can be assembled with other functional materials [84–86], and related derivatives can be synthesized by calcination and other methods [87–91]. In addition to porphyrins being applied to the framework materials as organic
ligands, porphyrins or metalloporphyrins can also be integrated into framework materials as guest molecules through microporous encapsulation and surface adsorption or graft, which are called porphyrins@framework material composites [92, 93]. After decades of development, porphyrin-based framework materials have been extensively studied and usually exhibit a diversity of functions. Porphyrin-based framework materials have been used in many catalytic reactions due to their simple synthesis and high chemical stability [94, 95]. Most reported examples of porphyrin-based organic linkers for framework materials are shown in Scheme 2.
Porphyrin-based framework materials as electro-/photocatalysts are introduced in this review, which lays a foundation for further research and development of porphyrin-based framework materials in the EC field. In addition, some challenges and future application prospects of porphyrin-based framework materials are also discussed.
## 2 Porphyrin-based framework materials as electrocatalysts
## 2.1 Electrocatalytic water splitting
Electrocatalytic water splitting is widely regarded as a promising renewable energy conversion method to produce efficiently high purity \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [96–100]. Electrocatalytic water splitting includes two basic reactions: electrochemical hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [101–108]. At present, platinum-based materials are the most effective HER electrocatalysts. At the same time the current OER process uses high-efficiency noble metal materials to reduce the high overpotential caused by the sluggish four-electron transfer [109–114]. However, due to its high cost and easy deactivation, this kind of precious metal is still inadequate in practical application [115–117]. A great deal of research has been done on low-cost metal-based OER electrocatalysts to replace precious metal electrocatalysts [118]. Due to the large specific surface areas and well-distributed active metal sites, MPFs/CPFs and related composites/derivatives have caught wide interest in many potential applications, for instance, electrocatalytic energy conversion [119, 120].
For pristine MPFs, Peng et al. [121], through a simple and controllable method, synthesized an OER bimetal electrocatalyst prepared from a two-dimensional (2D) Fe-based MPF precursor system for electrochemical water splitting (Fig. 1(a)). The unoccupied porphyrin center was arranged as a layer structure of HUST-8, which can be easily introduced into the second metal center through post-modification. The components and corresponding structures of HUST-8 are shown in Fig. 1(b). Due to the adaptation to the reaction conditions at room temperature, the porphyrin center in the layer frame was preserved. The relevant adsorption configurations have been set, and the stable adsorption configurations are shown in Fig. 1(c).
For MPFs composites, Samaneh Solarabi and colleagues [122] have synthesized a composite composed of cobalt-based MPF and multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNT), which had superior electrocatalytic activity for OER in water splitting. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of oxidized MWCNT, PCN-224, and PCN-224/MWCNT are shown in Fig. 1(d). Co porphyrins were located on the main chain of MPFs and were easy to contact with reactants as the active
center. The \(\mathrm{pH}\) dependence of the OER catalyzed by PCN-224 showed that the overpotential decreased with the increase of \(\mathrm{pH}\), as shown in Fig. 1(e).
MPF derivatives have also proven to be excellent electrocatalysts for water splitting due to the availability of good porosity and easy access to active sites [123]. Rong et al. [124] prepared a \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\)-Pt nanosheet HER electrocatalyst in situ on sulfur, nitrogen-doped carbon-based substrate (CuSNC) derived from rose-shaped Cu-TCPP MOF (Fig. 1(f)). In the Heyrovsky step, good hydrophilicity and a well-designed porous structure also promoted material transfer. They increased the collision probability between the active site and the intermediate \(\left(\mathrm{H}^{\star}\right)\), thus achieving fast HER efficiency (Fig. 1(g)). CuSNC@MoS2-Pt electrocatalysts possessed good electrocatalytic activity of HER in alkaline, and the Tafel slope was \(55.7\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\).
For pristine CPFs, porphyrins can be covalently or noncovalently modified [125]. Aisan Khaligh and co-workers [126] synthesized a novel CPF (COF-TPP-CB[6]), which was assembled by clicking perpropargyloxy cucurbit[6]uril (CB[6]) to azido-functionalized tetraphenylporphyrin (TPP-4N3) through the Cu-catalyzed azide-alkyne cycloaddition reaction (CuAAC), as shown in Fig. 1(h). The polarization curves of products are shown in Fig. 1(i), \(12\%\mathrm{Ni@TPP-CB[6]}\) as the best-performing electrocatalyst showed an impressive hydrogen production
rate of \(18.7\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}h^{-1}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) with the low onset potential of \(-250\;\mathrm{mV}\).
## 2.2 Electrocatalytic carbon dioxide reduction
Large amounts of carbon dioxide emissions contribute to global warming, causing serious damage to the ecological environment [127–133]. Hence strategies to reduce greenhouse gases emerge [134, 135]. Among the existing strategies, electrochemical carbon dioxide reduction \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\right)\) is a promising method due to its operating environment temperature and pressure, as well as by adjusting the application potential to effectively control the target product [136–144]. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) has a variety of pathways with different products, for instance, \(C_{1}\) \(\left(\mathrm{CO},\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right.\) and so on) [145–154], \(C_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH},\right.\) and so on) [155–160], \(C_{3}\) \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},\right.\) and so on) [160, 161]. In those pathways, the reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CO}\) (Eq. (1)) is one of the most promising methods at present because of its high selectivity and high current density, as well as the easy separation of gaseous products from liquid water.
\[
\mathrm{CO}_2(\mathrm{g})+2\mathrm{H}^++2\mathrm{e}^-=\mathrm{CO}(\mathrm{g})+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}E(\mathrm{CO}_2/\mathrm{CO})
\]
However, until now, only a few catalysts with high catalytic performance, including Au and Ag noble metals, have limited
the practical large-scale application of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [162–167]. The non-noble porphyrins and metalloporphyrin-centered molecular catalysts have been extensively studied due to their inherent large ring ligand structure and metallic center with adjustable oxidation state to reduce the overpotential and promote the conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO [168, 169]. Porphyrin-based framework materials, based on porphyrins and metalloporphyrins, have attracted people's interest due to their excellent electrocatalytic performance of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\).
For pristine MPFs, Nikolay Kornienko and colleagues [170] applied a electrochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) system by selecting MPFs
For pristine MPFs, Nikolay Kornienko and colleagues [170] applied a electrochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) system by selecting MPFs with the catalytic linking unit and preparing it into a thin film covering a conductive substrate. The appropriate catalytic linkage unit (Fig. 2(a)) was assembled into a porous film MPFs (Fig. 2(b)). Increasing the scan rate of cyclic voltammetry (CV) (Fig. 2(c)) illustrated that a cathodic wave centered roughly at \(-0.4\) to \(-0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). Detailed examination of a cobalt MPF, \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{TCCP-CO}\) demonstrated excellent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) performance with selectivity over \(76\%\) for CO production and stability over \(7\,\mathrm{h}\). For electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), Idan Hod and co-workers [171] used a material that contained functionalized Fe-porphyrins as
catalytically competents redox-conductive linkers, and used functionalized Fe-porphyrins to synthesize porphyrinic MOF-525 (Fig. 2(d)). CVs of homogeneous Fe-TPP in dimethyl formamide (DMF) at a scan rate of \(0.1\ \mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) are shown in Fig. 2(e). The chemical product of the reduction reaction was the mixture of CO and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) , and the CO faraday efficiency \(\mathrm{(FE_{CO})}\) was about \(100\%\) of the per-site turn-over number (TON) of 1,400. A three-dimensional (3D) highly stable PCN-222(Fe) MOF composed of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{e}}\) clusters connected by square-planar Fe-TCPP ligands was selected as the molecular catalyst studied under heterogeneous conditions (Fig. 2(f)) [172]. CV for the composite catalyst PCN-222(Fe)/C was first performed in a \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}\) solution saturated with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,8.4\) or \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.4\) (Fig. 2(g)). After electrolysis at \(-0.60\:\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\) , the crystallinity and stability of the catalyst remained unchanged and the average \(\mathrm{FE_{CO}}\) was \(80.4\%\) with the turnover frequency (TOF) of \(0.012\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) .
For porphyrins@MOFs composites, Zhang et al. [173] accurately anchored the catalytically active cobalt porphyrin on water-stabilized two-dimensional MOF nanosheets. Ultra-thin
two-dimensional MOF nanosheets [TCPP(Co)/Zr-BTB] with accessible catalytic sites were obtained for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) . The \(\mathrm{TCPP}(\mathrm{Co})/\mathrm{Zr}\) -BTB-PSABA (PSABA, \(\mathrm{P}\) -sulfamidobenzoic acid) exhibited the best performance with Faradaic efficiency \(\mathrm{(FE_{CO})}\) of \(85.1\%\) , TOF of \(5,315\ \mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{r}^{-1}\) and \(j_{\mathrm{total}}\) of \(6\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(-0.769\:\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE).
For pristine CPFs, research efforts have focused on changing the size and shape of the molecular building blocks to design the structural properties of CPFs, such as porosity [174–179]. By modifying their network structure, Christian S. Diercks et al. [180] adjusted the electronic properties of the porphyrin active site of electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in CPFs. The CPF layer grows perpendicular to the graphite layer rather than coplanar with the electrode surface (Fig. 2(h)). CVs of the samples are shown in Fig. 2(i). However, COF-366-(F)4-Co is the second electron-absorbing material, but has the lowest reactivity, which cannot fully represent this trend (Fig. 2(j)). For this phenomenon, perhaps due to the high hydrophobicity of the skeleton, there is less opportunity for the electrolyte to enter the active site [181, 182]. Moreover, An et al. [183] prepared a
For CPFs composites, Lin et al. [166] prepared a series of composite nanomaterials through directional polymerization of cobalt porphyrin unit and COFs template on aminofunctionalized CNTs for efficient electrocatalysis of CO2RR. Compared to pure COFs, the hybrid form of ultra-thin COFs nanolayers wrapped on CNTs can achieve a distended current density and stable \(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}\).
## 2.3 Electrocatalytic oxygen reduction
Fuel cells and metal-air batteries are one of the most promising devices among all the new generation energy conversion technologies that can meet our increasing energy needs [184–187]. However, the overall efficiency of these two excellent energy conversion devices is severely limited by oxygen reduction reactions (ORRs) [188, 189]. In ORR, molecular oxygen is electrochemically reduced to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in an acidic solution or \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)in alkaline solution via the four-electron pathway, or to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in acidic solution or \(\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{-}\)in alkaline solution as the intermediate species via the two-electron pathway [189–191]. Compared with the two-electron pathway, the four-electron pathway is preferred because of its higher kinetics and efficiency. In addition, platinum-based materials are ideal four-electron path ORR electrocatalysts in practical applications, but the wide application of ORR devices is limited by the scarcity, expensive and low durability of Pt catalysts [191–193]. Given these limitations, the development of alternative energy sources rich in earth resources, highly reactive, and durable, is a critical and challenging task. Porphyrin-based MOFs and COFs have proven to be highly competitive as electrocatalysts for ORR because of their high specific surface area, adjustable chemical composition, numerous active sites, and tunable pore size and topology [194].
For pristine MPFs, Lin et al. [195] synthesized four cubic zirconium porphyrin frameworks CPM-99(H2, Zn, Co, Fe) by using a molecular configuration-guided method. Among them, the addition of the mid-substituted side arms of the tetratopic porphyrin linkers successfully achieved a zirconium carboxylate skeleton with a cubic \(2.5\,\mathrm{nm}\) cage (Fig. 3(a)). For this work, Lin et al. [195] opened up a new way to prepare non-noble metal ORR catalysts by artificially stable metalloporphyrin MOFs. Pavel M. Usov and his colleagues [196] prepared the highly robust MOF constructed from \(Z\mathbf{r}_{2}\) carbonyl clusters and Fe(III) porphyrin linkers (PCN-223-Fe), as the electrocatalyst for ORR. The crystal structure of PCN-223-Fe MOF is shown in Fig. 3(b). Porphyrin ligands were linked to \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) carbonyl clusters to form highly stable structures, which made it particularly suitable for acidic ORR environments.
For MPFs derivatives, He et al. [197] synthesized a layered nanoframework of MPF assembled from nanosheets. Subsequent pyrolysis and acid etching of the material resulted in n-doped layered graphite-carbon nano frames (N-GCF) equipped with \(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) units (Fig. 3(c)). The diffusion-limited current density of N-GCF was \(6.19\,\mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\), and the half-wave potential was \(0.83\,\mathrm{V}\) compared with the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). Jiao et al. [198] exploited the dual protection strategy to obtain
high content of single-atom catalysts by nanocasting \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) into MPFs (Fig. 3(d)). The SEM showed that the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}\) had spindle morphology, and no particles were observed (Fig. 3(e)). \(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}\) demonstrated superior ORR performance in both alkaline and acidic environments.
For porphyrin@MOF composites, through ligand exchange, cobalt porphyrin can be grafted onto the surface of MOFs (Fig. 3(f)) [199–202]. On this basic, Lang and colleagues [203] prepared MOF-supported cobalt porphyrins (donated as \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZIF}-8\)) for electrocatalytic ORR with improved activity and selectivity (Fig. 3(g)). SEM images of \(1@ZIF-8\) are shown in Fig. 3(h). As shown in Fig. 3(i), the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) of \(1@ZIF-8\) showed an ORR wave with the onset potential at \(E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.83\,\mathrm{V}\) versus RHE (all potentials measured in aqueous solutions are referenced to RHE), and the half-wave potential at \(E_{1/2}=0.79\,\mathrm{V}\). Based on the porphyrin@MOF composites, some people are going to derivative them to prepare catalyst with better performance. Zhang et al. [204] conducted pyrolysis of ZIF-L &FeTPP@ZIF-8 to prepared FeCo-C/N (Fig. 3(j)). The pyrolyzed FeCo-C/N had the excellent ORR performance comparable to that of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) (20 wt. \(\%\) ), and the cost was significantly reduced.
For pristine CPFs, Yue and colleagues [205] prepared 4 kinds novel 2D CPFs (donated as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)-TP-COF, Co-TP-COF, Ni-TP-COF and Mn-TP-COF) with donor receptor pairs as electrocatalysts for ORR (Fig. 3(k)). Figure 3(l) showed the LSV curves of 4 kinds CPFs and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\). Among them, Co-TP-COF demonstrated the best ORR catalytic performance. In the four cases, different porphyrin centers of central atoms (H, Co, Ni and Mn) were the main catalytic centers.
## 3 Porphyrin-based framework materials as photocatalysts
## 3.1 Photocatalytic water splitting
The discovery of water-splitting photocatalysts dates back to 1972. Fujishima and Honda [206] discovered the semi-conductive properties of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), which can degrade water into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) under ultraviolet light. Based on this discovery, the application of semiconductor materials for photochemical water splitting has been studied extensively, including metal oxides, metal nitrides, and metal sulfides [207]. The principle of photocatalytic water splitting is shown in Fig. 4(a). However, these types of semiconductor materials still have great disadvantages, such as poor stability, low yield, low quantum efficiency, and most importantly, slow photocatalytic activity, unable to promote water splitting effectively [208–210]. The development of novel photocatalysts with high photocatalytic activity under visible light irradiation is one of the research hotspots of photocatalytic water splitting [211–216]. The central role of porphyrin groups in natural photosynthesis inspired the study, suggesting their application as molecular and supramolecular light collection systems [217]. On this basis, porphyrin-based framework materials have been extensively studied to apply in photocatalytic water splitting [218–220]. However, most research for porphyrin-based framework materials in photocatalytic water splitting has focused on MPFs and their related composites as potential next-generation semiconductors [221]. In addition, Z-scheme is a system that uses two kinds of photocatalysts to split water to produce hydrogen [222]. It is called the Z-Scheme reaction because of the Z-shape of electron transfer in this reaction process. The Z-scheme photocatalytic system
is an effective system for photodissociation of water and hydrogen production developed in recent years. It can transfer carriers under the impetus of an interfacial electric field and realize the improvement of quantum efficiency [223]. The working principle and schematic design of the Z-scheme photocatalytic system are shown in Fig. 4(b).
To convert light into fuel, photocatalytic MPFs and their composites have to absorb sunlight and transfer energy to a catalytic center for solar fuel production [224]. Koroush Sasan and co-workers [225] have synthesized a novel catalytic configuration based on direct coupling of organometallic \(\mathrm{[Fe_{2}S_{2}]}\) complex 1 with zirconium porphyrin MOF (Fig. 4(c)). For \(\mathrm{[FeFe]}@Z r\mathrm{PF},\) the rate of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production increased almost linearly for the first \(50\;\mathrm{min}\) , and then slowed down, reaching a stable level at about \(120\,\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. 4(d)). Li et al. [226] successfully
fixed \(\mathrm{Pt}\) -NPs in the limited coordination gap through the self-sensitized MPF of early transition metal Hf(IV) group clusters \(\mathrm{(Pt@Pd-PCN-222(Hf))}\) (Fig. 4(e)). Figure 4(f) demonstrated that \(\mathrm{Pt@Pd-PCN-222(Hf)}\) had excellent catalytic durability. In addition, Li et al. [226] also speculated on the mechanism of \(\mathrm{Pt@Pd-PCN-222(Hf)}\) HER reaction (Fig. 4(g)).
An excellent piece of work highlighted the layering of MPF composites into bionic hybrid assembly composites to accomplish complex functions. Hu et al. [218] have integrated HER-MOF and WOR-MOF (WOR = water oxidation reaction) nanosheets into liposome structures to separate the generated charges (Fig. 4(h)). HER-MOF nanosheets consisted of light-harvesting zinc porphyrins and catalytic platinum porphyrins and are functionalized by hydrophobic groups to facilitate their binding to the hydrophobic lipid bilayer of liposomes. WOR-MOF thin
slices are composed of \( [\mathrm{Ru}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipyridine})_{3}]^{2+} \) photosensitizer and Ir-bipyridine catalytic center located in the hydrophilic interior of liposomes. This liposome-MOF combination achieved overall photocatalytic water splitting with a significant quantum yield. In this work, complex electrocatalytic functions were accomplished by layering MOF components into bionic hybrid assembly components.
## 3.2 Photocatalytic carbon dioxide reduction
In nature, plants or microbes capture sunlight as an energy
supply and use it to convert carbon dioxide into energy carriers and oxygen. Inspired by natural photosynthetic systems, artificial photosynthesis is considered a promising method to achieve chemical energy conversion from solar energy to chemical energy, both to achieve energy regeneration and to mitigate the effects of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) on climate change [227]. The inorganic semiconductor materials mentioned above also contribute to photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) conversion [228, 229]. For photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \), these materials have a large band gap and limited light absorption range under visible light conditions, which is
not conducive to practical application [230, 231]. In recent years, the application of porphyrin-based framework materials in photocatalytic carbon dioxide reduction has been widely studied due to their inherently large surface areas, controllable void sizes, and high densities of catalytic active sites [232–234]. Furthermore, the Z-system is also suitable for the photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) system [235]. However, their total and half reactions are different.
For pristine MPFs, Zhang and colleagues [236] achieved modular optimization of MPFs by introducing coordination of unsaturated single atoms into the MPF matrix (Fig. 5(a)). The mechanism study showed that the existence of a single Co atom can greatly improve the efficiency of electron–hole separation of porphyrin units. MPFs composed of atomic catalytic center significantly improved the performance of photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). Fang et al. [237] synthesized an MPF
named PCN-601, which was composed of reactive nickel-oxo cluster nodes and light-trapping metalloporphyrin ligands linked by pyrazole groups, and catalyzed the photoreduction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) gas phase as a whole \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at room temperature. The rate of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) formation under PCN-601 was three times and twenty times higher than those under carboxylic MPFs and classical \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{CdS}\) photocatalysts, respectively. Kong et al. [238] proposed a strategy to improve the stability and functionality of unstable \(\mathrm{Zr-MPF}\) by in-situ porphyrin substitution. The schematic diagram of in situ ligand replacement strategy is shown in Fig. 5(b). In the absence of photosensitizers, the incorporation of metal porphyrins made BUT-110 MPF have high catalytic activity in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction process.
On the basis of MPFs, many people have explored the application of MPF composites in photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [239, 240]. In particular, the incorporation of metal nanoparticles (MNPs) in framework materials has been rapidly growing in the field of heterogeneous catalysis [241, 242]. Chen et al. [243] solved the problem that the interaction between light and matter would be greatly hindered by a \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) photocatalyst prepared by AuNPs loaded thin porphyrin paddle-wheel frameworks-3 (PPF-3) nanosheets (PPF-3_1) through electrostatic interaction. The MPF composites supported by AuNPs showed excellent photocatalytic activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion to HCOOH through plasma resonance energy transfer process. The mechanism for plasma enhanced photocatalytic activity of PPF-3_1 for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is shown in Fig. 5(c). In addition, doping various functional substances into MPFs is also a reasonable and effective way to improve photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) performance. Sunghan Choi and colleagues [244] chemically doped \(\mathrm{PCN}{-}222(\mathrm{Zn})\) with carboxylic acid anchor group containing Re(I) molecule to prepare the novel MOF-Re(I) photocatalyst (Fig. 5(d)). The high catalytic activity was mainly due to the effective exciton migration and chimney transfer of photoexcited porphyrin linker to the peripheral Re(I) catalytic site.
Semiconductor nanocrystals in the size range from 2 to \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) are often referred to as quantum dots (QDs) and have received significant attention due to their uniquely tunableness and size-dependent electronic properties [245, 246]. Zheng et al. [247] synthesized a novel hybrid catalyst by coordinating zero-dimensional (OD) carbon nitride QDs (G-CNQDs) with 2D ultrathin MPFs (Fig. 5(e)). For the prepared hybrid catalyst, the generation rates of CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) were 16.10 and \(6.86~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), respectively. Furthermore, Wu et al. [248] encapsulated \(\mathrm{CN}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) \(\left(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\right)\) perovskite QDs in the pores of \(\mathrm{PCN}{-}221(\mathrm{Fe}_{x})\) by sequential deposition method and constructed a series of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}@\mathrm{PCN}{-}221(\mathrm{Fe}_{x})\) composite photocatalysts. Using \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}@\mathrm{PCN}{-}221(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2})\) as the photocatalyst for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), The total yield of the product composed of CO \((34\%)\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) \((66\%)\) was \(1,559\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which was a record high.
\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is an N-type semiconductor with a wide band gap \((3.0{-}3.2\ \mathrm{eV})\). Due to its low price, thermodynamic invariance, and environmentally friendly characteristics, it has been widely studied in recent decades [249, 250]. Wang et al. [240] established an effective photocatalytic system through the direct integration of \(\mathrm{PCN}{-}224(\mathrm{Cu})\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles, which can greatly improve the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction. The evolution rate of the prepared catalyst CO was \(37.21\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\). Moreover, Wang et al. [251] coordinated \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) tetra(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (CuTCPP) into the UIO-66 MOF structure via a one-pot synthesis strategy. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{CuTCPP}\subset\mathrm{UIO}{-}66/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) composite \(\left(\mathrm{CTU}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)\) was obtained by the in-situ growth of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles on MOFs. The proposed photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) pathway over \(\mathrm{CTU}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is shown in Fig. 5(f). The catalytic results showed that the optimum evolution amount of CO was \(31.32\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\).
was obtained by the in-situ growth of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles on MOFs. The proposed photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) pathway over \(\mathrm{CTU}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is shown in Fig. 5(f). The catalytic results showed that the optimum evolution amount of CO was \(31.32\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) .
COFs with \(\pi\) conjugated organic groups (such as porphyrins or phthalocyanines) have special properties of semiconductors and can be used for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [252]. Wang and colleagues [253] synthesized \(\mathrm{Cu@CPFs}\) nanorods using solvothermal design and then electrodeposited \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanowires onto free porphyrin-CoFs on carbon fibers (Fig. 5(g)). The \(\mathrm{Cu@CPFs}\) nanorods showed high catalytic activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and gave C1–C3 chemicals. Hydroxyl groups in radical CPFs played an important role in the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\).
## 4 Summary and outlook
At present, the development of efficient electro-/photo-catalysts plays an important role in alleviating energy shortages. Porphyrin-based framework materials have broadened application prospects in electrocatalysis and photocatalysis due to their abundant metallic active sites, adjustable crystalline structure, and high specific surface area. Herein, we summarize and introduce recent research progress of porphyrin-based framework materials (porphyrin-based MOFs/COFs) in electro-/photo-catalytic water splitting, electro-/photo-catalytic carbon dioxide reduction, and electro catalytic oxygen reduction. Different molecular porphyrin structures are shown as building units through schematic diagrams. The catalytic activity of porphyrins can be regulated by the metal active center and surrounding functional groups. Due to the diversity of metal cluster nodes and organic connectors, porphyrin frameworks have different topologies, morphologies, and pore sizes. In addition, we have classified the different combination modes of porphyrins and frame structures: (1) Porphyrins are directly applied to the framework materials as organic ligands; (2) porphyrins integrate into framework materials as guest molecules through microporous encapsulation and surface adsorption or graft.
Porphyrin-based framework materials combining the advantages of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts become candidate materials for related electron analysis. The catalytic activity of porphyrin-based framework materials is mainly determined by the properties of porphyrins. In addition, electron transfer and mass diffusion efficiency also have a great influence on catalytic activity. On the basis of pristine porphyrin-based framework materials, the enhancement of electron transfer was realized by combining porphyrin framework materials with conductive materials by physical or chemical methods, in situ growing porphyrin-based framework materials on conductive supports, calcinating porphyrin-based framework materials into porous carbon materials at high temperatures. Porphyrin-based framework materials have large pore size, open-pore channel, and high porosity, thus improving the mass transfer performance. Although a great deal of research has been done on porphyrin-based framework materials as efficient electrical/photocatalysts, many challenges remain, as follows:
(1) The development of efficient non-precious porphyrin-based framework material catalysts to replace precious metal catalysts remains a significant challenge, and much effort is required to modify precursors of framework materials for specific applications of electro-/photo-catalysis. In addition, the
electro-/photo-catalytic properties of porphyrin-based framework materials are far from reaching the actual industrial level.
(2) In MOFs/COFs, the design and construction of porphyrin blocks mainly rely on high-symmetry guided design, which limits the diversity of porphyrin framework families and affects their potential catalytic applications. Understanding the structure–activity relationship of porphyrin framework materials is of great significance for the design and synthesis of porphyrin-based framework materials.
(3) In practical application, it is very important to enhance the structure and long-term mechanical stability of porphyrin-based framework materials due to harsh catalytic conditions. In addition, partially decomposed porphyrin-based MOFs should focus on further research to understand the reaction mechanism and the true active site. It is difficult to obtain the precise crystal structure of porphyrin-based COFs prepared by the one-pot method, so the influence of amorphous porphyrin-based COFs on their catalytic activity and reaction mechanism is still a great challenge.
(4) Due to the high cost of preparing porphyrin framework materials, it is urgent to develop new synthesis methods. As the cost of porphyrin-based framework materials is an important factor to be considered in large-scale industrial applications, reducing the synthesis steps is also an important research direction in the future. However, it is extremely difficult to develop new synthesis paths and reduce synthesis steps.
Although porphyrin-based framework materials have the above challenges at present, with further research, a variety of strategies can be adopted to solve these challenges.
(1) The design and development of novel porphyrin-based framework materials can significantly enhance their catalytic activity by increasing the exposure of catalytic active sites. Firstly, the mass transfer process of the porphyrin-based framework materials can be enhanced by adjusting the porosity exposed to more active sites. Secondly, the electron transfer process can be enhanced by introducing of electron collection and donor nodes through the construction of conductive porphyrin-based framework materials by growing porphyrin-based framework materials on conductive substrates. Thirdly, the intrinsic catalytic activity can be directly increased by creating fewer defects and adjusting the coordination number of the metal in porphyrin-based framework materials. Fourthly, the large planar structure of porphyrin blocks guarantees strong \(\pi\) interaction with guest molecules. The coupling of porphyrin-based framework materials with another guest can lead to their synergistic properties in confined pore environments and enhance their catalytic performance.
(2) Novel structures can be constructed by introducing porphyrin units with asymmetric substituents and nodes with abnormal connection numbers. In addition, novel porphyrin-based framework materials with various porosities and topological structures can be prepared by combining hybrid porphyrin units with flexible organic linkers.
(3) The thermal and chemical stability of porphyrin-based MOFs can be enhanced by using highly oxidized metal central nodes (e.g., \(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+}\) ). \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -based porphyrin-MOFs, especially \(Z\mathbf{r}\) chain nodes, are usually very stable in strong acidic and alkaline solutions. The stability of MOFs in weak acids and bases can be improved by adding - \(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\) groups into the pores. In addition, porphyrin-based COFs usually have high chemical stability due to covalently linked organic ligands, and more research can be focused on this.
(4) COF-367-Co nanosheets can be prepared on a large scale with high yield by using a simple and low-cost imine exchange strategy [254]. This strategy can be applied to the construction of other bonded porphyrin-based COFs with good application prospects.
Some novel catalysts are also being studied extensively. Single atom catalysts (SACs), such as Co-porphyrin, are promising candidates for photocatalysts due to their high atomic efficiency and excellent catalytic activity [255–258]. In addition, there is also nickel ferrocyanide as a high-performance electrocatalyst for urea oxidation [259].
In summary, porphyrin-based framework materials remain a largely unexplored area of research. Further research will likely reveal new parameters and new applications, and these materials are likely to flourish in electro-/photo-catalysis fields in the coming years.
